# What each of this deployment's services may use, in the container of its own
# that it runs in. Copy to var/containers.conf; var/, because the figures
# belong to one machine.
#
# Every service but the tower runs in a container, and needs a line here: a
# service with no line does not start, and the refusal names the service and
# the missing limit (scripts/run-service.sh, README "Services in containers").
# There is no native start to fall back to.
#
# Both limits, always. A container given neither gets 4 CPUs and 1 GiB
# whatever the service is, so a line that states only one of them is refused
# by name. CPUs are a whole number; memory is a number and M or G, and
# Apple's container refuses less than 200M.
#
# The figures are this Mac mini's (64 GiB, 18 cores; design/measured.md,
# "The final limits", 2026-11-25), set from what each service was seen to use:
#
#   flows   the tape rebuild holds a whole day in memory. A day with trades
#           and quotes for nine instruments peaked at 6.5 and 8.1 GiB in a
#           container, the hourly projection's three-day shape at 8.2 GiB, and
#           a lane given 4G was killed, not slowed. The limit follows the largest day
#           in the window, not the window's length.
#   nats    27 MiB in use, serving capture and the status subjects. A replay
#           window on the bus will need its own figure.
#   capture 210 MiB in use for nine instruments; 2G leaves room for the cap of
#           fifty, if what it holds grows with the instruments.
#   ledger  not measured: no ledger is declared here. Measure it before it is
#           relied on, and before this line is copied.
#
# With galata-vault's container (1G) and galata-watcher's two (1G each), the
# deployment's containers may hold 17.25G together, and 29.25G while
# scripts/build-images.sh's builder (12G) is up.
#
# service              cpus  memory
flows                  4     12G
nats                   1     256M
capture:hyperliquid    2     2G
# ledger:hyperliquid   2     2G
